        <h2 id="faq" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How long do whiplash symptoms last?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Recovery time varies widely. Mild whiplash — no imaging findings, minimal symptoms — typically resolves fully within 4 to 6 weeks with conservative care. Moderate whiplash can take 3 to 6 months. Severe whiplash involving disc herniations or nerve involvement may take 6 to 18 months or longer. Research suggests that approximately 10% to 15% of whiplash patients develop chronic long-term symptoms lasting more than a year. You should never settle your claim until your symptoms have stabilized and your doctor has confirmed you have reached Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI).
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Can I get a whiplash settlement without going to the doctor?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Technically possible for the most minor claims, but practically very difficult. Without medical records linking your symptoms to the accident, insurance companies will deny or drastically minimize your claim. There is no objective evidence of your injury without a doctor's diagnosis. Even for mild whiplash, visiting an urgent care clinic or your primary physician within 24 to 48 hours of the accident is essential — both for your health and your claim.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Do I need an MRI for a whiplash claim?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              You do not legally require an MRI, but having one can dramatically increase the value of your claim if it shows objective findings. X-rays only show bones — soft tissue damage is invisible on X-ray. An MRI can reveal herniated discs, ligament tears, or nerve compression that transforms a "soft-tissue only" claim into a documented, objectively verifiable injury. If you have symptoms that go beyond surface-level muscle soreness — especially arm numbness, tingling, or weakness — push for an MRI referral.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How is whiplash different from other neck injuries?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Whiplash specifically refers to the mechanism of injury — the rapid back-and-forth motion — and the resulting soft-tissue damage to the cervical spine. More serious "neck injuries" include herniated cervical discs (which can be a result of whiplash but involve structural disc damage), cervical fractures (broken vertebrae), and spinal cord injury. The distinction matters for settlements: pure soft-tissue whiplash with no structural findings is worth significantly less than whiplash that caused disc herniation or nerve damage.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Is chiropractic treatment covered in a whiplash settlement?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg>
              </span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">
              Yes — chiropractic treatment bills count as economic damages and are included in your settlement calculation. However, insurance adjusters tend to discount chiropractic bills more heavily than bills from MDs, orthopedic surgeons, or neurologists. They may argue that chiropractic care was excessive or that you should have transitioned to physical therapy sooner. To counter this, have your chiropractor document why each course of treatment was medically necessary and ensure your treating MD supports the chiropractic recommendation in their records.
            </p>
          </details>
        </div>

        <p>
          Whiplash may be "common," but that does not make it simple or low-value. With the right documentation and a clear understanding of how your claim is calculated, a moderate to severe whiplash injury can produce a settlement that genuinely compensates you for the pain, disruption, and recovery you have endured.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-10 p-5 border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-lg text-[10px] text-mute leading-relaxed">
